00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:01.000
MARTIN OMANDER: Here's what
Anna's prototype looks like.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.980
From this web page,
users should be

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.830
able to sign-in using their
identity provider of choice.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.470
Once they have signed
in, they should

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.630
see their recorded hikes.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.840
Users should only be able
to see their own hikes.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.840
CHRIS WILCOX: Anna reads up on
Firebase Auth, which supports

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.800
multiple identity providers.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.315
It also includes a default user
interface called Firebase UI.

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:24.190
MARTIN OMANDER: The code
for Anna's prototype

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.360
is linked in the
description below.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.690
Let's look at the
most important pieces.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.340
CHRIS WILCOX: In
her JavaScript code,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.710
Anna initializes
Firebase UI, which

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.710
will display one button for
each identity provider shown

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:36.790
to the user.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:39.400
She also tells the
Firebase UI library

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.870
which page to redirect
to after the user has

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.420
signed in successfully.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:46.630
Finally, she renders the
Firebase UI component.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.140
MARTIN OMANDER:
On the next page,

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.770
the one that users are
redirected to when they log

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:54.820
in successfully, Anna
calls the getIdToken().

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:58.300
The string she gets back is a
signed JSON web token, which

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.820
contains the user's unique ID.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.710
CHRIS WILCOX: After
fetching the ID token,

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.780
Anna's code requests
the user's hikes

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.510
by calling the
server-side REST API.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.840
Her code includes the ID token
and the authorization header

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:11.830
of the call.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.360
MARTIN OMANDER: As
we saw last time,

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.140
Anna implemented the
server-side REST API in Node.js

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.940
running on Cloud Run.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.670
Her server-side code
reads the JSON web token

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.270
from the authorization header
and extracts the user's ID

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.220
from it.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.070
Her server-side code
then uses this ID

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:33.610
to ask the database for that
particular user's hikes.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:34.720
CHRIS WILCOX: But wait.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.080
How does Anna verify that
the JSON web token is valid?

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.660
And how does she extract
the user ID from it?

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.550
As mentioned in
our previous video,

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.280
Anna has limited experience
with cryptography.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.990
MARTIN OMANDER: That's where
the Firebase SDK comes in.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.920
If Anna imports this package
into her server-side code,

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.120
it has a method called
verifyIdToken() that does just

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:55.880
that.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.620
It verifies the token and
extracts the user ID from it

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.840
if it is valid.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.760
If the token is invalid--

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.950
for example, if a malicious user
is attacking Anna's REST API--

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.960
the verifyIdToken()
call will not succeed,

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.300
and Anna's code can
return an error.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.590
CHRIS WILCOX: That's neat.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.690
Anna has now
secured her REST API

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.270
so users can only
access their own hikes,

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.070
and she did it without
having to become

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:22.510
an expert in cryptography.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.850
The libraries did most
of the heavy lifting.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.350
MARTIN OMANDER:
That's right, Chris.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:29.770
It's always important to use
tested and reliable packages

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.648
